      "teaches": "Each dot is one molecule with an identity, a mass, and a typical speed — the macroscopic gradient is just the bookkeeping of many such particles.",
      "label": "Hover a molecule"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two gases, one barrier",
      "narration": "The stopcock is closed: every white H2 molecule is trapped in the left bulb and every red O2 molecule in the right. Each gas sits on a maximum concentration gradient with nowhere to go.",
      "state": {
        "stage": "(a) closed",
        "timeSlider": 0,
        "massMode": "Plain colors"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Open the valve",
      "narration": "With the bore clear, molecules begin wandering across. Notice the asymmetry: several white dots have already reached the right bulb while only a few red dots have made it left.",
      "state": {
        "stage": "(b) just opened",
        "timeSlider": 25,
        "massMode": "Plain colors"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why H2 leads",
      "narration": "Switch to the mass-coded view: H2 at 2 g/mol carries long fast trails, O2 at 32 g/mol short slow ones. At the same temperature the lighter gas has the higher average speed, so it spreads roughly four times faster.",
      "state": {
        "stage": "(b) just opened",
        "timeSlider": 40,
        "massMode": "Mass-coded speeds"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep time forward",
      "narration": "Drag the time slider and watch the counters: net transfer slows as each gradient shrinks, even though individual molecules never stop moving.",
      "state": {
        "stage": "(b) just opened",
        "timeSlider": 70,
        "massMode": "Mass-coded speeds"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Uniform mixture",
      "narration": "Long after opening, both bulbs contain the same blend of white and red. Molecules still cross the stopcock constantly, but now equally in both directions — equilibrium, not stillness.",
      "state": {
        "stage": "(c) equilibrium",
        "timeSlider": 100,
        "massMode": "Plain colors"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Diffusion needs no pump — random motion alone erases a concentration gradient, and because lighter molecules move faster at the same temperature, H2 invades the O2 bulb faster than O2 invades the H2 bulb.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single horizontal apparatus centered in a wide canvas: two equal pale blue-green spheres with a blue-gray outline and a soft light highlight in the upper-left of each, joined at their equators by a short tube carrying a schematic stopcock (barrel, oval handle plate on top, small stem below) drawn in the same blue-gray. Above the left bulb the label 'H2', above the right 'O2', with proper subscripts. Molecules are small filled circles: H2 pure white with a faint gray edge, O2 bright red. At time 0 the left bulb holds ~30 white dots scattered at random and the right bulb ~32 red dots, both filling their spheres evenly with no overlap and none inside the tube. As time advances, dots migrate across the tube; at intermediate time the right bulb should visibly contain more white intruders than the left contains red ones. At full time both bulbs show an even salt-and-pepper mix of roughly equal counts. Caption text sits centered below the apparatus in plain dark gray, with the panel letter on a second line. In mass-coded mode, give each H2 dot a long faint motion trail and each O2 dot a short one, and show a small legend reading 'H2 2 g/mol — fast' and 'O2 32 g/mol — slow'. Add a small live counter under each bulb listing white and red totals, and a faint dashed vertical divider at the stopcock. Hovering a dot pops a small tooltip naming the molecule and its molar mass. Keep the palette limited to the pale teal glass, blue-gray outlines, white, and red so the two gases read instantly."
}

